PUBLIC RESPONSE: A

Under Ontario securities laws as of January 2021 (National Instrument 45-106), an accredited investor includes, among others:
- An individual with net financial assets exceeding $1,000,000;
- An individual with net income exceeding $200,000 individually, or $300,000 combined with a spouse, in each of the two most recent years and a reasonable expectation of the same in the current year;
- An individual with net assets of at least $5,000,000;
- A person other than an individual or investment fund with net assets of at least $5,000,000 as shown on its most recently prepared financial statements.

Option A describes a limited partnership. Although its limited partners (Liam, Jack, and Ace) and its general partner holding company may individually qualify as accredited investors, the limited partnership itself is not automatically an accredited investor merely because its partners are wealthy. The limited partnership would need to satisfy an entity-based test, such as having net assets of at least $5,000,000 on its own financial statements. No such net asset figure is provided for the partnership itself, and there is no general “all owners are accredited” category under the Ontario definition. Therefore, the limited partnership would not be classified as an accredited investor based on the facts given.

Options B, C, D, and E each satisfy an applicable accredited investor category (income test, individual net asset test, or corporate net asset test), so A is the correct answer.

